WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
The first film traced which changes can reach offspring.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.840
If an allele is dominant, must most individuals carry it?

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.140
Watch a few blue cards move through this group.

4
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.690
An allele is a version of a gene.

5
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.150
In this simple model, an individual receives one copy from each parent.

6
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.690
The pair is its genotype.

7
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.830
The trait that appears is its phenotype.

8
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.850
Dominant and recessive describe how the pair of allele copies affects that

9
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:34.850
phenotype.

10
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.970
A dominant allele shows its trait even when only one copy is present.

11
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.630
A recessive allele shows its trait only when both copies are recessive.

12
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.970
So a dominant allele can mask a recessive allele in one individual.

13
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.070
Neither word means stronger, healthier, or better.

14
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.850
Only a few blue allele cards are in this group.

15
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:03.510
When one blue card enters a genotype pair, will its trait show?

16
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.490
Decide before the window changes.

17
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:14.913
The blue card enters, and the trait shows.

18
00:01:16.153 --> 00:01:18.653
That is dominance inside this individual.

19
00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:23.473
Yet the population bowl still contains very few blue cards.

20
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:27.233
The rule for showing changed the phenotype window.

21
00:01:28.453 --> 00:01:30.653
It did not make the allele common in the group.

22
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:35.727
Now move more blue cards into the bowl without changing the showing rule.

23
00:01:37.047 --> 00:01:38.207
The group display rises.

24
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:40.807
Move them out, and it falls.

25
00:01:42.127 --> 00:01:45.067
The individual display still follows the allele pair.

26
00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:48.087
Frequency belongs to the group.

27
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:52.407
Dominance belongs to the relationship between alleles.

28
00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:55.587
A dominant trait may be common or rare.

29
00:01:56.747 --> 00:01:59.447
A recessive trait may also be common or rare.

30
00:02:00.727 --> 00:02:02.067
These are separate questions.

31
00:02:03.427 --> 00:02:06.227
One asks what an allele does beside another allele.

32
00:02:07.547 --> 00:02:10.747
The other asks how many copies exist across a whole group.

33
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.810
The everyday word dominant can sound like winning.

34
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.770
Genetics uses it more narrowly.

35
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:23.390
It describes how a phenotype appears when different alleles share a genotype.

36
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.950
It does not say one allele will spread, replace another, or become common.

37
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.450
Population frequency is a separate pattern to measure.

38
00:02:34.533 --> 00:02:39.253
To test a claim about commonness, count traits or alleles in a defined group.

39
00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:45.593
To test dominance, follow which phenotypes appear in offspring, as Mendel did with

40
00:02:45.593 --> 00:02:49.753
peas. One kind of evidence cannot replace the other.

41
00:02:51.093 --> 00:02:54.933
Later courses add inheritance patterns beyond simple dominance.

42
00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.713
So when you hear dominant, look inside one genotype pair.

43
00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:03.553
Ask whether a copy shows beside a different allele.

44
00:03:04.853 --> 00:03:07.353
When you hear common, look across the whole group.

45
00:03:08.613 --> 00:03:11.073
Ask how often the allele, or its trait, occurs.

46
00:03:12.453 --> 00:03:14.653
Never use one answer for both questions.
